{"kind":"assert","text":"Second correction to planner assert 66f8b350 (replacing the request-time fence in a8ae5232), per the checker: R-MINT-6 runs a token's TTL from when Artifacts applies the create, and nothing bounds that application time, so no request-time fence exists. The rule for the fork sweep (R-WS-3) under request 02836f9a is therefore: on a fork that has any ForkTokens record in the unknown state, the sweep revokes only tokens whose ids are known (recorded by the fork ledger or by a lease) and leaves every other active token alone, for as long as that record stays unknown; the record itself settles only by its own create answer or an actually documented completion or attribution guarantee from the provider, never by time or inventory. The exposure is bounded by the token's own TTL from application (R-MINT-6), and the report states this as the trade: on such forks R-WS-3's cleanup of unlisted tokens is suspended, not weakened by inference. The normative received-id rule for R-MINT-3: once an id is received it is written durably before any revoke is attempted; a failed write is retried, bounded, with the id still in hand; if storage refuses every bounded retry the Room cannot guarantee the duty survives it and the record's earlier state governs, which the amendment says plainly; the existing failed-write clause otherwise applies only before an id is known."}